{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "purpose": "simulation Phase 3 (#841): sm.setgain(mode='fbm') and sm.setleakage then sm.corrupt, run by CASA and by casa-rs",
  "notes": [
    "Each corrupted scenario predicts a 1 Jy unpolarised point at the phase centre, so its noise-free twin's parallel hands are 1 and its cross hands 0; compare_sm_ms.py compares DATA against the twin by statistics (corruption_stats.py), as the draws differ.",
    "A one-element amplitude [a] is CASA's complex amplitude (a, a): the gain screens' scale is |(a, a)| = a·sqrt(2) ('gain_scale'), and a leakage's real and imaginary parts are drawn with standard deviations amplitude[0] and amplitude[1] ('leakage_amplitude').",
    "With autocorrelations written (CASA's default), CASA's fill loop leaves the last antenna with unit gain ('casa_unit_antennas'), which casa-rs does not follow (docs/CASA (C++) bugs.md).",
    "CASA 6.7.6's fBM screens copy their last two or three slots from past the end of their FFT's output and normalise with them, so their amplitude and phase scales vary from run to run (0.15 to 1 of the documented scale measured): 'casa_gain_checks' checks only CASA's closure, casa-rs is held to the documented scales, and the two implementations' screens are compared by their autocorrelations (docs/CASA (C++) bugs.md).",
    "corrupt-gain-noautos fits the gains from cross-correlations alone, and its last antenna appears only as ANTENNA2.",
    "CASA draws one leakage per spectral window for the whole run (its infinite solution interval spans scans and fields) and simulates only the first two windows, leaving later ones uncorrupted, which casa-rs does not follow (docs/CASA (C++) bugs.md). corrupt-leakage therefore observes two windows, four scans each; it has no autocorrelations, which would give CASA's last antenna a leakage of 1."
  ],
